  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 AM CST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nWith our winter system off to the east, brief weak surface high\npressure has built into the region today. Expect some sky \nclearing this afternoon and slightly warmer temperatures into \nthe 50s, as warmer air is advected into the region with our \nsoutherly surface winds. Unfortunately, cloud cover will return \nby late this evening ahead of the next upper trough, as the \nsurface low moves in. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms are expected to begin overnight from \nwest to east ahead of the cold front and continue into Sunday \nafternoon. Instability continues to remain unimpressive with \nMUCAPE maxing between 200-300J/kg. However, surface lapse rates \nhave trended slightly upward since last night to 6-6.5C/km \nacross some models with dewpoints potentially reaching the low \nto mid 50s. Otherwise, mid-level lapse rates continue to sit \nbetween 6-7.5C/km and 0-6km bulk shear remains impressive with \n50-65kts. This environment will be sufficient for some \nthunderstorms to develop with these showers. In general we \nexpected most storms to remain sub-severe, however, there is the\npotential for some of the late morning and early afternoon \nstorms to have some stronger damaging wind gusts and the \npotential for a brief spinup tornado due to the strong speed and\ndirectional shear (0-1km SRH between 200-300m2/s2) and low \nheights associated with these storms. With this environment in \nplace, the Storm Prediction Center has issued a Marginal risk \nfor western KY and far southern portions of IN.\n\nBehind these storms and post-cold front, we expect a windy Sunday\nafternoon and evening. Expect sustained winds between 15-25mph \nwith gusts up to 35-45mph. Currently, the REFS suggests a spread\nof 30-80% of wind gusts above 40mph across the Quad State. It \nis possible that a Wind Advisory may be issued for these winds \nas a result. Any outdoor decorations or furniture should be tied\ndown or put away.\n\nSurface high pressure will return next week with another warming\ntrend. Highs are expected to reach the low 60s by Wednesday and\ncontinue to warm into the mid 60s Thursday. Weather will remain\ndry after Sunday until late Thursday when another surface low is\nforecast to bring more rain to the region.",
